          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I find the age of a Bradford White water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Read the first two letters of the serial number. The first is the year, on a 20-year cycle that skips I, O, Q, R, U and V, and the second is the month, A through M without I. DG is July 2007, for example. Choose the cycle by the heater's appearance, or confirm the date with Bradford White using the full serial."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where is the serial number on a water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On the rating label, a sticker on the side of the tank usually about two-thirds of the way up, near the gas control on a gas model or the upper access panel on an electric one. It lists the model, serial number, capacity and input rating. If the heater is against a wall, a phone camera held behind it can usually read it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I read a Rheem water heater serial number?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Skip any leading letters and read the first four digits. The first two are the month and the next two are the year, so 0815 means August 2015. That rule applies to Rheem, Ruud and Richmond tank water heaters. Rheem furnaces and air conditioners use a different format, so do not apply the water heater rule to them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I read an A.O. Smith water heater serial number?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On heaters built from 2008 onward, the first two digits are the year and the next two are the week of manufacture, so 1532 is week 32 of 2015. Earlier units used a letter for the month followed by a two-digit year. If you get a week above 52 or an implausible date, contact A.O. Smith with the full serial."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I look up my water heater's age online?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many manufacturers offer warranty or product lookup tools on their websites that take the serial number, and their support lines will confirm a date by phone. Third-party decoder sites exist, but they rely on the same patterns described here and are not always right for older or unusual formats."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How old is too old for a water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Gas tanks commonly last 8 to 12 years and electric tanks 10 to 15. Past that range, a heater is living on borrowed time, and planned replacement is worth considering, especially if it sits above finished space. Age alone does not mean it will fail tomorrow, but it does mean expensive repairs rarely make sense."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does the manufacture date affect the warranty?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Manufacturers typically start the warranty from the manufacture date unless you can show a proof of installation, such as a receipt or invoice, and registering the heater can extend or clarify coverage. A heater that sat in a warehouse for months may have less warranty left than you think, so keep your paperwork."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
