    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Your water heater almost certainly knows how old it is. The date is printed on the rating label or hidden in the first few characters of the serial number, and once you know which brand made the tank, decoding it takes about a minute. The catch is that each manufacturer uses its own code, a few of them reuse letters on a 20-year cycle, and reading the wrong format can put you a decade off. Here is how to get it right.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="rating-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start With the Rating Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every tank water heater sold in North America carries a rating plate, usually a sticker on the side of the jacket about two-thirds of the way up, near the gas control or the upper element panel. It lists the brand, model number, serial number, capacity, fuel or voltage and input rating. On a heater tucked into a closet, the label is often facing the wall, so a phone camera held around the back saves you from moving anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for a printed date before you decode anything. Many newer labels state the manufacture date outright, sometimes as Mfg Date or Date of Manufacture, and some include a month and year next to the serial. If you find one, that is your answer and it beats any decoding chart. Older labels, and many contractor-brand labels, rely on the serial number alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write down, or photograph, the full serial number exactly as printed, including any leading letters. The difference between a letter O and a zero, or a letter I and a one, matters for some brands. If the label is faded or peeling, a flashlight held at a low angle often brings the print out. Do not scrape it with anything sharp; you can lose characters you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note the brand too, and be aware that the name on the front is not always the company that built the tank. Water heaters in the United States come mostly from three manufacturers, Rheem, A.O. Smith and Bradford White, and each sells under several names. Our guide to <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands</a> shows which names belong to which family, which tells you which date code to try.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORTCUT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A printed date on the label beats every chart.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Decoding charts are compiled from manufacturer guidance and inspector experience, and formats have changed over the decades. If the label prints a date, use it. If it does not, decode the serial, then confirm with the manufacturer if the answer decides whether you replace the heater.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="rheem-ruud" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Rheem, Ruud and Richmond: Month, Then Year
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rheem-built tank water heaters, including the Rheem, Ruud and Richmond names, generally put the date in the first four digits of the serial number, after any leading letters. The first two digits are the month and the next two are the year. A serial that begins Q0815 or 0815 reads as August 2015. One that begins 1103 reads as November 2003.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ignore the letters at the start. Rheem serials commonly open with one or two letters that identify the plant or product line, and they are not part of the date. Start counting at the first digit. If the first two digits are 13 or higher, you are probably reading the wrong positions, because there is no 13th month; try starting one character later, or check whether the label carries a separate date.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be careful not to apply the Rheem water heater rule to a Rheem furnace or air conditioner. Rheem and Ruud HVAC equipment has used a different format in which a letter F is followed by a week and then a year, so the same four digits can mean something different. If you are dating a whole mechanical room, decode each appliance with its own product's rule.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Serial begins</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reads as</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Q0815…</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">August 2015</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leading letter ignored</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">0794…</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">July 1994</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two-digit year, judge the century by appearance</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1103…</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">November 2003</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Month first, then year</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The two-digit year only tells you the year within a century, so 0798 could in principle be July 1998 or a recent unit, but in practice a heater from 1998 looks nothing like a current one. The label design, the jacket and the energy guide sticker make the century obvious. If you still have doubts, Rheem's customer support can confirm the date from the full serial number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ao-smith-state" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. A.O. Smith, State and American: Year, Then Week
          </h2>

          <p>
            A.O. Smith reverses the order and uses weeks rather than months. On heaters built from 2008 onward, the serial number is an 11-character string whose first two digits are the year and the next two are the week of manufacture. A serial beginning 1532 means week 32 of 2015, which is early August. One beginning 2105 means the fifth week of 2021, late January or early February.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before 2008, A.O. Smith used a different format with a letter for the month followed by a two-digit year, where A is January, B is February and so on. For example, H06 in the serial reads as August 2006. Third-party guides disagree about exactly which letter in older serials is the month when there are two letters at the start, so treat a pre-2008 decode as an estimate and confirm it with A.O. Smith if it matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            State water heaters, now part of the A.O. Smith family, generally follow the same year-then-week pattern on recent units. Older State heaters used their own formats, and some units built at former State plants after the merger still differ. If the A.O. Smith rule produces a nonsense result, such as a week number above 52, try the State format or call the manufacturer.
          </p>

          <p>
            American Water Heater and the brands it built, including many units sold under retailer names, are also inside the A.O. Smith family. Their serials are widely read the same way, with the first two digits as the year and the next two as the week. A serial starting 1104 would be the fourth week of 2011. As always, a printed date on the label overrides the decode.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bradford-white" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Bradford White: The Letter Code That Repeats Every 20 Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bradford White is the brand that sends most people searching, because its date is in letters rather than numbers. The first letter of the serial is the year and the second letter is the month. A serial that starts DG reads as July 2007: D for 2007 and G for July. The rest of the serial is a sequential production number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The year letters skip I, O, Q, R, U and V to avoid confusion with numbers and each other, which leaves 20 letters. That means the code repeats every 20 years: A was used for 1984, again for 2004 and again for 2024. You have to judge which cycle fits the heater in front of you. A 2005 heater and a 1985 heater look very different, and by the 1980s cycle most heaters have long since been replaced.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Year letter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Year</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Year letter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Year</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1984 / 2004 / 2024</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">L</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1994 / 2014</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">B</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1985 / 2005 / 2025</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">M</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1995 / 2015</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">C</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1986 / 2006 / 2026</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">N</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1996 / 2016</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">D</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1987 / 2007</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">P</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1997 / 2017</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">E</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1988 / 2008</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">S</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1998 / 2018</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1989 / 2009</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">T</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1999 / 2019</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">G</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1990 / 2010</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2000 / 2020</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">H</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1991 / 2011</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">X</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2001 / 2021</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">J</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1992 / 2012</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Y</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2002 / 2022</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1993 / 2013</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Z</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2003 / 2023</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The month letter runs A through M and skips I, so A is January, H is August, J is September, K is October, L is November and M is December. A serial that starts SJ is September 1998 or September 2018, and a heater with a modern label and a current-style energy guide sticker is the 2018 one. A serial starting ML is November 1995 or November 2015.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bradford White's customer support can confirm a date from the serial number, and that is worth using if the result sits right on the boundary between repair and replace. Because Bradford White sells only through contractors, the plumber who installed it may also have records. Many newer Bradford White labels also print the manufacture date, which removes the guesswork entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ge-whirlpool" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. GE, Whirlpool and Other Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            GE and Whirlpool are appliance brands, not tank manufacturers in the way Rheem or A.O. Smith are, and their water heaters have been built by outside makers under license. That is why their serial formats are less consistent, and why the most reliable step for either brand is to check the label for a printed date or call the brand's support line with the full serial number.
          </p>

          <p>
            For GE-branded tank heaters, the format most often cited by inspectors puts the month in the first two digits and the year in the next two, after any leading letters, so 0312 would read as March 2012. That is the same month-then-year pattern Rheem uses. Some older GE and Hotpoint appliances used a two-letter code instead, with each letter mapping to several possible years. If the four-digit reading does not produce a sensible month, contact GE Appliances support rather than guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whirlpool water heaters are commonly reported to share American Water Heater's coding, with the first two digits as the year and the next two as the week. A serial starting 0128 would be the 28th week of 2001. Whirlpool does not publish a consumer-facing decoder for its water heaters, so treat this as a strong estimate and confirm with support if it matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            For any other brand, including store brands and older names that no longer exist, the approach is the same. Find the label, look for a printed date, then try the year-and-week and month-and-year patterns on the first four digits after any letters. Whichever one produces a valid week or month and a year that matches the look of the heater is usually right. If neither does, the manufacturer's support line or a plumber familiar with the brand is your next stop.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
