        <div id="no-serial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When the Serial Number Will Not Tell You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sometimes the label is gone, painted over or unreadable, or the serial follows a format nobody can decode. You can still get a reliable estimate from paperwork. Check your home inspection report, which usually lists the water heater's age, and any closing documents or seller disclosures from when you bought the house. Inspectors date heaters routinely, so the report often has the answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for an installer's sticker on the jacket or the gas line. Many plumbers leave a sticker with their name and the installation date, and if they do not, a call to the company named on any service tag may turn up the job record. Your local building department may also have a permit on file, since many jurisdictions require a permit to replace a water heater, and the permit date is close to the install date.
          </p>

          <p>
            The heater's physical details can narrow it down too. In April 2015, updated federal efficiency standards took effect that made many tank models, especially those over 55 gallons, larger or required heat pump or condensing technology. A thick, modern jacket, an electronic gas valve or a recent-style yellow EnergyGuide label all point to a newer unit. Heavy rust at the base, a flaking jacket and an old-style label point the other way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the manufacturer can almost always help if you can read even part of the serial and the model number. Warranty departments decode serials every day, and if you registered the heater when it was installed, they may also be able to tell you how much tank warranty remains. That is worth knowing before you plan any repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-age-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What the Age Means for Repair or Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know the date, compare it with typical lifespans. Gas tank heaters commonly last about 8 to 12 years and electric tanks about 10 to 15, with water quality, maintenance and anode condition doing most of the work. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> covers what shortens and extends those numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age changes the math on repairs. Replacing a thermocouple, a drain valve or a relief valve makes sense on almost any heater. Paying for a gas control valve or a major repair on a tank that is already 12 years old usually does not, because the tank itself is close to the end and the money is better spent on the replacement. A heater that is leaking from the tank is done at any age; our guide to a <a href="/water-heater-leaking/">water heater leaking</a> explains how to tell a tank leak from a fixable one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Age also tells you whether to replace on your schedule or wait for a failure. A tank past ten years sitting above a finished floor, in an attic or in a closet next to a bedroom is a reasonable candidate for planned replacement, because a sudden failure there can do more damage than the heater costs. One sitting on concrete near a floor drain with a leak alarm next to it can be run longer with less risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the date says it is time, plan the budget before you shop. Our breakdown of <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost</a> explains what installers typically charge and what adds to the bill, and our guide to the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> compares the models we would choose for a replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the leading letters as part of the date</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On Rheem, GE and many other brands, the letters at the start of the serial identify the plant or product line. Counting them throws the whole decode off. Start at the first digit, and if you get a month above 12 or a week above 52, you are reading the wrong positions or the wrong brand's format.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using one brand's chart for another</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rheem reads month then year; A.O. Smith reads year then week. Read a Rheem serial starting 0815 with the A.O. Smith rule and August 2015 becomes week 15 of 2008, seven years off. Confirm the brand and its parent family before decoding, because the name on the front is not always the maker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Bradford White 20-year cycle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bradford White year letters repeat every 20 years, so an S can mean 1998 or 2018. Use the heater's condition, label style and any paperwork to choose the right cycle. A rusty, worn tank with an old-style label is not the recent one, however tempting that answer is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a decode over a printed date</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the rating label prints a manufacture date, use it. Decoding charts are compiled from manufacturer guidance and inspector experience, and formats have changed over the decades. A printed date or confirmation from the manufacturer's support line beats any chart, including ours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing manufacture date with install date</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The serial gives the build date. Heaters can sit in a warehouse for months before installation, so the install date is usually somewhat later. For warranty claims, manufacturers commonly use the manufacture date unless you can show proof of installation, so keep your receipt or invoice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I find the age of a Bradford White water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Read the first two letters of the serial number. The first is the year, on a 20-year cycle that skips I, O, Q, R, U and V, and the second is the month, A through M without I. DG is July 2007, for example. Choose the cycle by the heater's appearance, or confirm the date with Bradford White using the full serial.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where is the serial number on a water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the rating label, a sticker on the side of the tank usually about two-thirds of the way up, near the gas control on a gas model or the upper access panel on an electric one. It lists the model, serial number, capacity and input rating. If the heater is against a wall, a phone camera held behind it can usually read it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I read a Rheem water heater serial number?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Skip any leading letters and read the first four digits. The first two are the month and the next two are the year, so 0815 means August 2015. That rule applies to Rheem, Ruud and Richmond tank water heaters. Rheem furnaces and air conditioners use a different format, so do not apply the water heater rule to them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I read an A.O. Smith water heater serial number?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On heaters built from 2008 onward, the first two digits are the year and the next two are the week of manufacture, so 1532 is week 32 of 2015. Earlier units used a letter for the month followed by a two-digit year. If you get a week above 52 or an implausible date, contact A.O. Smith with the full serial.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I look up my water heater's age online?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many manufacturers offer warranty or product lookup tools on their websites that take the serial number, and their support lines will confirm a date by phone. Third-party decoder sites exist, but they rely on the same patterns described here and are not always right for older or unusual formats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How old is too old for a water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas tanks commonly last 8 to 12 years and electric tanks 10 to 15. Past that range, a heater is living on borrowed time, and planned replacement is worth considering, especially if it sits above finished space. Age alone does not mean it will fail tomorrow, but it does mean expensive repairs rarely make sense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the manufacture date affect the warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Manufacturers typically start the warranty from the manufacture date unless you can show a proof of installation, such as a receipt or invoice, and registering the heater can extend or clarify coverage. A heater that sat in a warehouse for months may have less warranty left than you think, so keep your paperwork.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

