          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The age of a water heater is on its label. Look for a printed date first, then decode the serial with the right brand's rule: month then year for Rheem, year then week for A.O. Smith and its family, and the 20-year letter cycle for Bradford White. For GE, Whirlpool or anything unusual, confirm with the manufacturer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Once you have the date, compare it with the typical 8 to 12 years for gas and 10 to 15 for electric, and let it decide how much repair is worth paying for. If the answer is replacement, our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best gas water heaters</a> guide is the place to start.
